---
// /guides — the task guides, one card each, one anchored section each.
//
// The route is one of the four the design handoff locks, and the header, the
// footer, the docs sidebar and the library's empty state all link it. Titles,
// blurbs and source lines come from GUIDES in src/data/nav.ts so a guide cannot
// be listed in one place and written in another.
//
// Every command and number below is taken from the project's own files, named
// at the end of each guide: the pet tree's README.hermes.md and docs/evopet/,
// and the library's README.md. Nothing here is a hosted-service claim.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { GUIDES, LIBRARY_API_DEFAULT } from '../../data/nav';

/** One guide's metadata, looked up so the card and the section cannot drift. */
function guide(id: string) {
  const found = GUIDES.find((entry) => entry.id === id);
  if (!found) throw new Error(`no such guide: ${id}`);
  return found;
}

const [chooseGates, publishPet, allProfiles, floatDesktop, readState] = GUIDES;

const publishEndpoint = `POST ${LIBRARY_API_DEFAULT}/api/pets/publish   # multipart/form-data, bearer key
GET  ${LIBRARY_API_DEFAULT}/api/manifest       # the public index, approved pets only`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Guides"
  description="Five EvoPet task guides: choose your evolution gates, publish a pet, install across all profiles, float the pet on the desktop, and read your pet's state — each with what it needs, what to expect, and how it fails."
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <p class="docs-kicker">Guides</p>
    <h1>Task guides</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Five things you will actually do with EvoPet: what each needs first, the steps in order, what
      a correct run looks like, and how it fails. Every command and number comes from the project's
      own files, named at the end of each guide.
    </p>

    <ul class="guide-list">
      {
        GUIDES.map((entry) => (
          <li class="guide-card">
            <h2>
              <a href={`#${entry.id}`}>{entry.title}</a>
            </h2>
            <p>{entry.blurb}</p>
            <div class="guide-meta">
              <span class="mono-label">{entry.id}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <section class="guide" id={chooseGates.id}>
      <h2>{chooseGates.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{chooseGates.blurb}</p>

      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>A checkout of the pet tree. The pet needs only Python: Pillow ships with Hermes.</li>
        <li>
          Clear on who chooses: EvoPet fixes the ladder; <em>you</em> fix how many times your pet
          changes form and at which levels.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Steps</h3>
      <ol class="guide-steps">
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>1. Pick levels, not XP amounts</h4>
          <p>
            A gate is a <em>level</em> your pet reaches; its cost is whatever the fixed curve says
            that level is worth. One to four gates, strictly increasing, levels 1&ndash;99. Keep the
            first gate above level 1: a gate at level 1 fires at 0 XP, so the pet starts already
            hatched and its first form is never drawn.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>2. Price the gates in turns</h4>
          <p>
            <code>xp_for_level(L) = round(100_000 &times; ((L &minus; 1) &divide; 98)&sup2;)</code>,
            and a good turn is about 18 XP. Gates at 20, 40 and 60 cost 3,759, 15,837 and 36,245 XP
            &mdash; about 209, 880 and 2,014 turns. Gates below level 30 feel fast; gates above level
            50 do not.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>3. Declare them in the manifest</h4>
          <p>
            In the <code>pet.json</code> the install will replace:
            <code>{'"evopet": { "evolutionGates": [20, 40, 60] }'}</code>. Form names are positional,
            not yours to choose: <code>egg</code>, <code>hatchling</code>, <code>child</code>,
            <code>teen</code>, <code>adult</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>4. Install, which is when the gates take effect</h4>
          <p>
            The installer absorbs the manifest it is about to replace into the ledger
            (<code>pet_compiler.sync_ledger_gates</code>), so the new <code>pet.json</code> carries
            the ledger's gates: the running pet evolves on the ledger's copy, not the manifest.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>5. Check the table before you ship the pet</h4>
          <p>
            The gate control on <a href="/docs#gates">the mechanics page</a> recomputes the table for
            1&ndash;4 gates from the published curve, and <code>levels.gate_report(gates)</code>
            prints the same table from the same formula.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="expected">
        <span class="note-title">What a correct run gives you</span>
        <p>
          Gates at 20, 40 and 60 give four forms &mdash; egg, hatchling, child, teen; three gates
          cannot reach adult, by design. The default pet's gates, 11/23/32/45, are 1,041, 5,040,
          10,006 and 20,158 XP, matching the gate control on the mechanics page. An installed gate
          becomes a ledger floor, and XP only rises, so a pet cannot regress out of a form.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="failure">
        <span class="note-title">How it fails</span>
        <p>
          A block present but invalid is refused at install, and the error names the file:
          <code>pet.json: evolution gates must strictly increase; 20 follows 40</code>. Out of range
          (&gt;99), not increasing, zero gates, or more than four gates all fail the same validator.
          Silence is not a declaration, so <code>{'"evopet": {}'}</code> leaves installed gates
          alone. A line missing a form your gates imply is not caught here; it fails later, when the
          pet grows into it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sources"><strong>Source.</strong> {chooseGates.source}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide" id={publishPet.id}>
      <h2>{publishPet.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{publishPet.blurb}</p>

      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>A library service to publish to; its routes are at <a href="/docs#endpoints">the endpoints</a>.</li>
        <li>
          An owner key, minted once with <code>npm run key:issue -- --email you@example.com --username you</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          A package: <code>pet.json</code> (&le;16&nbsp;KB), <code>spritesheet.webp</code> or
          <code>.png</code> at exactly 1536&times;1872 (8&times;9 cells of 192&times;208), and
          optionally a thumbnail (&le;256&nbsp;KB, &le;512&times;512).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Steps</h3>
      <ol class="guide-steps">
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>1. Mint the key, and keep the refresh slot empty</h4>
          <p>
            A key looks like <code>evk1.&lt;id&gt;.&lt;secret&gt;</code> and is stored hashed: the
            secret is never written, logged or returned again. The command prints a paste-ready blob
            that writes <code>{'{"access_token":"evk1…","refresh_token":""}'}</code>. Leave
            <code>refresh_token</code> empty: a library key in the refresh slot is a credential leak.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>2. Declare capability as an object</h4>
          <p>
            <code>{'"evopet": { "evolutionGates": [11, 23, 32, 45] }'}</code>: evolution capability
            is the object-valued <code>evopet</code> key and nothing else, and declared gates are
            validated exactly as the pet validates them.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>3. Publish</h4>
          <pre><code>{publishEndpoint}</code></pre>
          <p>
          The publish is <code>multipart/form-data</code> with a bearer key: parts
          <code>pet.json</code>, <code>spritesheet.webp</code>, and optionally
          <code>thumbnail.webp</code> and <code>public=false</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>4. Read the response</h4>
          <p>
            <code>{'{slug, status, url, capable, evopet, thumbnailUrl, version, replaced, notes}'}</code>.
            With no flag the status is <code>approved</code> and the package is listed immediately.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>5. Confirm it is listed</h4>
          <p>
            <code>GET /api/manifest</code> &mdash; public, no auth, approved pets only, sorted by
            slug. The same index this site's <a href="/library">library page</a> reads.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="expected">
        <span class="note-title">What a correct run gives you</span>
        <p>
          The packet is in <code>/api/manifest</code> with <code>capable: true</code> and its parsed
          <code>evopet</code> block, and it renders in the library page and the desktop app's library
          panel. <code>public=false</code> is the only thing that produces <code>pending</code>.
          Deleting is <code>DELETE /api/pets/&lt;slug&gt;</code> (owner or operator): it removes the
          index record and every package object, leaving a <code>404</code> rather than a tombstone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="failure">
        <span class="note-title">How it fails</span>
        <p>
          Validation happens before anything is stored. A manifest that does not parse, an
          <code>id</code> that is not a legal slug (<code>[a-z0-9-]</code>, 1&ndash;64 bytes, no
          leading or trailing dash), a <code>displayName</code> over 96 bytes, or a sheet that is not
          the documented atlas all fail the publish, and nothing is written. A string
          <code>{'"evopet": "yes"'}</code> is refused outright, and a <code>pending</code> package's
          bytes requested by anyone but its owner return <code>404</code>, not <code>403</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sources"><strong>Source.</strong> {publishPet.source}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide" id={allProfiles.id}>
      <h2>{allProfiles.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{allProfiles.blurb}</p>

      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>A checkout of the pet tree on the machine that runs Hermes.</li>
        <li>
          <code>HERMES_HOME</code> understood: unset means <code>~/.hermes</code> (the default
          profile); inside a named profile it is <code>&lt;home&gt;/.hermes/profiles/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Steps</h3>
      <ol class="guide-steps">
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>1. Install once, in the profile you are in</h4>
          <p>
            <code>./hermes/install-hermes.sh --line toast --machine aurora</code>, then
            <code>hermes plugins enable tamahermes</code>, <code>hermes pets select tamahermes</code>
            and <code>hermes pets doctor</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>2. Then cover every profile at once</h4>
          <p>
            <code>./hermes/install-all-profiles.sh</code> &mdash; the default profile plus
            <code>~/.hermes/profiles/*</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>3. Narrow it if you need to</h4>
          <p>
            <code>--only name1,name2</code> installs into some profiles, and
            <code>--keep-selection</code> installs the pet without changing which pet is active.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>4. Or one home at a time</h4>
          <p>
            <code>HERMES_HOME=~/.hermes/profiles/you ./hermes/install-hermes.sh</code>, and
            <code>HERMES_HOME=~/.hermes ./hermes/install-hermes.sh</code> for the default profile.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="expected">
        <span class="note-title">What a correct run gives you</span>
        <p>
          Each profile owns its own <code>&lt;HERMES_HOME&gt;/pets/tamahermes/pet.json</code>, its own
          compiled atlas and its own ledger, and <code>hermes pets doctor</code> reports the pet
          ready. That means the pet is profile-scoped: &ldquo;all profiles&rdquo; is one pet
          <em>per profile</em>. Making those many pets one creature is the combined ledger's job, the
          last guide here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="failure">
        <span class="note-title">How it fails</span>
        <p>
          If <code>hermes plugins enable</code> dies with
          <code>TypeError: list indices must be integers</code>, that profile still has the legacy
          bare-list form of <code>plugins:</code> in its <code>config.yaml</code>; rewrite it to the
          mapping form and retry. If you move the checkout, the repo root recorded at
          <code>&lt;HERMES_HOME&gt;/tamahermes/repo-root</code> goes stale: re-run the installer, or
          set <code>TAMAHERMES_REPO_ROOT</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sources"><strong>Source.</strong> {allProfiles.source}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide" id={floatDesktop.id}>
      <h2>{floatDesktop.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{floatDesktop.blurb}</p>

      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>
          The desktop shell installed on this machine. It is a local build today: there is no
          notarized download, and a bundle built on one Mac is rejected by Gatekeeper on another.
        </li>
        <li>The pet installed in the profiles whose work you want the floating pet to reflect.</li>
        <li>The desktop app <strong>closed</strong> &mdash; this is not optional.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Steps</h3>
      <ol class="guide-steps">
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>1. Quit the app first</h4>
          <p>
            The app rewrites its settings file from memory when it quits, so an activate pass against
            a running app is clobbered a moment later.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>2. Mirror the pet into the desktop home</h4>
          <p>
            <code>./hermes/install-hermes.sh --petdex-activate</code> pins it as the pet on screen;
            <code>--petdex</code> alone adds it to the rotation without pinning it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>3. Do it from every profile</h4>
          <p>
            <code>./hermes/install-all-profiles.sh --petdex-activate</code>. The mirror is
            machine-wide while ledgers are per-profile, so installing from every profile is what
            keeps the floating pet current from all of them.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>4. Or drive the mirror directly</h4>
          <p>
            <code>python -m tamahermes petdex --petdex-home ~/.petdex --force --activate</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="expected">
        <span class="note-title">What a correct run gives you</span>
        <p>
          <code>~/.petdex/pets/tamahermes/</code> holds <code>pet.json</code> and
          <code>spritesheet.webp</code>, the same 8&times;9, 192&times;208 atlas Hermes renders,
          because both read the one sheet. With <code>--activate</code>, the app's
          <code>active_pet</code> points at it and its other settings keys, in order, are preserved.
          Once the opt-in marker exists at
          <code>&lt;HERMES_HOME&gt;/tamahermes/petdex-home</code>, every growth refresh re-mirrors the
          sheet, so the floating pet evolves with the terminal one rather than freezing at install
          day.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="failure">
        <span class="note-title">How it fails</span>
        <p>
          Activating against a running app appears to work, then is overwritten. The mirror is a
          copy, not a second pet: it never grows on its own, and an unmanaged mirror shows whichever
          profile last changed. If hooks look dead, check the hook server's <code>/health</code>: the
          shell exits 0 by design when that server fails to bind, then keeps running with every hook
          silently doing nothing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sources"><strong>Source.</strong> {floatDesktop.source}</p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide" id={readState.id}>
      <h2>{readState.title}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{readState.blurb}</p>

      <h3>Before you start</h3>
      <ul class="prose">
        <li>An installed pet, from <a href="#install-across-all-profiles">the previous guide</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Steps</h3>
      <ol class="guide-steps">
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>1. Ask the pet</h4>
          <p>
            <code>python -m tamahermes --target hermes status</code>, or add
            <code>--json</code> for the machine-readable form. It reports the display name and the
            form it resolves to, the stage and level, XP, the five stats, the traits, and the visual
            state the atlas is drawn from.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>2. Read the profile's ledger</h4>
          <p>
            <code>&lt;HERMES_HOME&gt;/tamahermes/state.json</code>. This is the pet's own source of
            truth, and it is also where the installed gates live &mdash; which is why editing a
            manifest needs an install to take effect.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>3. Read the combined ledger</h4>
          <p>
            <code>~/.evopet/state.json</code>: one pet's worth of XP from every source, with the
            per-profile ledgers kept as the components they are. Its top-level fields include the
            mirror claim, which is what makes exactly one writer own the desktop mirror.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="guide-step">
          <h4>4. Fill it with the drain</h4>
          <p>
            <code>python -m tamahermes.evopet_drain</code> is a dry run by default;
            <code>--apply</code> writes the combined state and prunes the consumed spool, and
            <code>--no-mirror</code> drains without touching the desktop mirror. Defaults: spool{' '}
            <code>~/.petdex/runtime/evo-queue</code>, state <code>~/.evopet/state.json</code>,
            consumed events <code>~/.evopet/consumed</code>, each overridable with{' '}
            <code>EVOPET_SPOOL</code>, <code>EVOPET_STATE</code> and <code>EVOPET_CONSUMED</code>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="expected">
        <span class="note-title">What a correct run gives you</span>
        <p>
          A dry run reports what it would absorb and changes no bytes; a second run with no new
          activity reports zero deltas, the honesty check on the whole thing. The Hermes share of the
          combined ledger's XP equals the sum of the per-profile ledgers, and the clamped stats
          (energy, health, bond, mess, satiety) merge by maximum, not sum. A first run has a zero
          cursor, so each profile's whole history lands exactly once.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="failure">
        <span class="note-title">How it fails</span>
        <p>
          Nothing is written without <code>--apply</code>. Two spool events are ignored by design:
          posts with no <code>session_id</code> (shell hooks, display-only and un-attributable) and
          anything from Hermes, already counted by the profile ledger. The ledger carries numbers
          only, never your prompts, a path or a transcript, and a test holds that. One honest gap:
          every non-Hermes event seen so far has been a fixture, so until Claude Code and Codex really
          post to the hook server, the combined ledger earns from the Hermes route and little else.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sources"><strong>Source.</strong> {readState.source}</p>
    </section>

    <nav class="anchor-links" aria-label="Section navigation">
      <a href="/docs#reference">← Reference</a>
      <a href="/library">Browse the library →</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
